    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) are recognized as equal, effective first-line options for managing primary hypertension, though ARBs are generally preferred due to a more favorable side-effect profile.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) are recognized as equal, effective first-line options for managing primary hypertension"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Comparison"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Efficacy: ACE inhibitors and ARBs lower blood pressure and reduce major cardiovascular events (such as heart attack and stroke) with comparable effectiveness.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects & Tolerability:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "are better tolerated and have a lower discontinuation rate."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent, dry, hacking cough (due to the accumulation of bradykinin) in a notable minority of patients."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Both classes carry a risk of hyperkalemia (elevated potassium) and acute kidney injury, and both are strictly contraindicated during pregnancy."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Angioedema (swelling of the lips, tongue, or throat) can occur with both classes, though it is significantly less common with ARBs than with ACE inhibitors.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  2,
                  3,
                  4,
                  5
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Therapy: Never use an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together. Dual blockade of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system does not improve cardiovascular outcomes and sharply increases the risk of dangerous side effects like high potassium and kidney failure .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Choosing Between Them"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Start with an ARB when initiating new therapy if you want to minimize the risk of developing an ACE-inhibitor-induced dry cough."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Switch to an ARB if you are currently taking an ACE inhibitor and develop a chronic dry cough or mild side effects."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Specific Clinical Contexts: While both are interchangeable for general hypertension, ACE inhibitors have a long history of dedicated mortality trials in reduced-ejection-fraction heart failure (HFrEF), whereas ARBs serve as the standard substitute when ACE inhibitors cannot be tolerated.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2,
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "For comprehensive educational overviews on medication options, you can consult the Cleveland Clinic Guide to Antihypertensives or read the summary on GoodRx Losartan vs. ARB Comparisons .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
